1
00:00:00,240 --> 00:00:02,800
A textbook model says a soda can should be

2
00:00:02,800 --> 00:00:04,480
as wide as it is tall.

3
00:00:04,480 --> 00:00:06,400
The can in your hand is almost twice as

4
00:00:06,400 --> 00:00:07,760
tall as it is wide.

5
00:00:07,760 --> 00:00:10,640
What changes when we model a real can?

6
00:00:10,640 --> 00:00:12,560
Let's find the textbook answer with one

7
00:00:12,560 --> 00:00:15,360
derivative, then change the assumptions.

8
00:00:15,360 --> 00:00:17,440
Here's a standard twelve ounce can.

9
00:00:17,440 --> 00:00:19,360
It's filled with about three hundred fifty

10
00:00:19,360 --> 00:00:21,520
five milliliters, and it's about six point

11
00:00:21,520 --> 00:00:23,760
six centimeters wide and twelve point two

12
00:00:24,000 --> 00:00:26,800
tall. But it isn't a simple cylinder.

13
00:00:26,800 --> 00:00:28,880
It has empty space at the top, a narrow

14
00:00:28,880 --> 00:00:30,880
neck, and a domed base.

15
00:00:30,880 --> 00:00:33,440
A plain cylinder this wide, holding

16
00:00:33,440 --> 00:00:34,880
exactly three hundred fifty five

17
00:00:34,880 --> 00:00:37,360
milliliters, would be only about ten point

18
00:00:37,360 --> 00:00:39,280
four centimeters tall.

19
00:00:39,280 --> 00:00:41,680
The textbook model is simpler: a closed

20
00:00:41,680 --> 00:00:43,840
cylinder, with the same thickness of metal

21
00:00:43,840 --> 00:00:46,000
everywhere, holding a fixed volume.

22
00:00:46,000 --> 00:00:48,560
Unroll it, and you get two circles, each

23
00:00:48,640 --> 00:00:51,840
pi r squared, and a rectangle two pi r

24
00:00:51,840 --> 00:00:53,520
wide and h tall.

25
00:00:53,520 --> 00:00:56,240
So the metal area is two pi r squared,

26
00:00:56,400 --> 00:00:58,000
plus two pi r h.

27
00:00:58,450 --> 00:00:59,880
The volume is fixed at three hundred fifty

28
00:00:59,890 --> 00:01:01,970
five cubic centimeters.

29
00:01:01,970 --> 00:01:05,090
Volume is pi r squared h, so once you pick

30
00:01:05,090 --> 00:01:09,010
r, the height is set: h equals V over pi r

31
00:01:09,010 --> 00:01:11,730
squared. Substitute, and the area depends

32
00:01:11,730 --> 00:01:15,250
on r alone: two pi r squared, plus two V

33
00:01:15,410 --> 00:01:16,530
over r.

34
00:01:16,530 --> 00:01:18,690
Now graph that area against the can's

35
00:01:18,690 --> 00:01:20,930
width, the diameter, which is two r.

36
00:01:20,930 --> 00:01:22,760
A very narrow can needs a lot of side

37
00:01:22,770 --> 00:01:26,290
wall. A very wide can needs huge ends.

38
00:01:26,290 --> 00:01:28,930
So the area rises on both sides, and in

39
00:01:28,930 --> 00:01:31,080
between there's a lowest point, about two

40
00:01:31,090 --> 00:01:33,570
hundred seventy eight square centimeters.

41
00:01:33,570 --> 00:01:36,370
The derivative finds that point exactly.

42
00:01:36,370 --> 00:01:38,770
The derivative is the slope of the curve,

43
00:01:38,770 --> 00:01:40,370
and at the bottom of this valley, the

44
00:01:40,370 --> 00:01:41,650
slope is zero.

45
00:01:41,650 --> 00:01:44,130
The derivative of the area is four pi r

46
00:01:44,210 --> 00:01:46,370
minus two V over r squared.

47
00:01:46,370 --> 00:01:49,330
Set it to zero, and V equals two pi r

48
00:01:49,330 --> 00:01:50,130
cubed.

49
00:01:50,130 --> 00:01:52,690
But V is also pi r squared h.

50
00:01:52,690 --> 00:01:55,730
So pi r squared h equals two pi r cubed,

51
00:01:55,730 --> 00:01:57,570
and h equals two r.

52
00:01:57,570 --> 00:01:59,330
Height equals diameter.

53
00:01:59,660 --> 00:02:01,420
For three hundred fifty five milliliters,

54
00:02:01,420 --> 00:02:03,260
that's about seven point seven centimeters

55
00:02:03,420 --> 00:02:04,460
each way.

56
00:02:04,460 --> 00:02:06,620
Now give our simple cylinder the same

57
00:02:06,620 --> 00:02:08,620
width as the real can, six point six

58
00:02:08,620 --> 00:02:10,540
centimeters. It needs only about two

59
00:02:10,700 --> 00:02:12,220
percent more surface area than the

60
00:02:12,220 --> 00:02:13,420
textbook optimum.

61
00:02:13,420 --> 00:02:15,820
Near a minimum, the curve is almost flat,

62
00:02:15,820 --> 00:02:18,140
so a fairly wide range of widths costs

63
00:02:18,140 --> 00:02:19,660
very little extra.

64
00:02:19,660 --> 00:02:21,500
Now a thought experiment.

65
00:02:21,500 --> 00:02:23,820
What if each end uses more metal per

66
00:02:23,820 --> 00:02:25,660
square centimeter than the side?

67
00:02:25,660 --> 00:02:27,500
Real lids are made of thicker metal than

68
00:02:27,500 --> 00:02:28,860
the thin body wall.

69
00:02:28,860 --> 00:02:31,100
In our model, the two ends are flat, full

70
00:02:31,100 --> 00:02:33,740
width disks, and each uses k times as much

71
00:02:33,740 --> 00:02:35,420
material per square centimeter as the

72
00:02:35,420 --> 00:02:38,380
side. We'll try k equals two point four,

73
00:02:38,460 --> 00:02:39,500
as an example.

74
00:02:39,500 --> 00:02:42,380
The weighted area is k times two pi r

75
00:02:42,540 --> 00:02:44,780
squared, plus two pi r h.

76
00:02:44,780 --> 00:02:47,250
The same derivative gives h equals two k

77
00:02:47,260 --> 00:02:50,540
r. Height over diameter equals k.

78
00:02:50,540 --> 00:02:53,100
With k equal to two point four, the best

79
00:02:53,260 --> 00:02:55,340
can is about five point seven centimeters

80
00:02:55,420 --> 00:02:57,500
wide and thirteen point eight tall.

81
00:02:57,710 --> 00:02:59,070
Taller and thinner.

82
00:02:59,070 --> 00:03:01,470
Here are both models, each measured as

83
00:03:01,470 --> 00:03:03,550
percent above its own minimum.

84
00:03:03,550 --> 00:03:05,950
The real can's diameter falls between the

85
00:03:05,950 --> 00:03:08,590
two modeled optima, and in both models

86
00:03:08,670 --> 00:03:10,510
it's only about two percent above the

87
00:03:10,510 --> 00:03:11,550
minimum.

88
00:03:11,550 --> 00:03:13,230
Now look at a real can.

89
00:03:13,230 --> 00:03:15,550
The body narrows before it meets the lid,

90
00:03:15,550 --> 00:03:17,230
and the base is domed.

91
00:03:17,230 --> 00:03:19,390
A real design also has to hold internal

92
00:03:19,390 --> 00:03:21,710
pressure, and run on filling and seaming

93
00:03:21,710 --> 00:03:23,950
equipment. Our models don't capture all

94
00:03:23,950 --> 00:03:25,790
that. What they show is how the

95
00:03:25,790 --> 00:03:27,870
assumptions change the answer.

96
00:03:27,870 --> 00:03:30,270
With uniform thickness, the cylinder that

97
00:03:30,270 --> 00:03:32,510
uses the least material is as tall as it

98
00:03:32,510 --> 00:03:35,230
is wide. Make its ends more material

99
00:03:35,230 --> 00:03:37,710
intensive, and the optimum gets taller.

100
00:03:37,710 --> 00:03:39,630
And because the curve is nearly flat

101
00:03:39,630 --> 00:03:41,870
around its minimum, a range of widths

102
00:03:41,870 --> 00:03:44,110
works with only a small material penalty,

103
00:03:44,270 --> 00:03:45,390
in the model.

104
00:03:45,390 --> 00:03:47,230
If you liked this video, please give it a

105
00:03:47,230 --> 00:03:48,990
like and subscribe for more everyday

106
00:03:48,990 --> 00:03:50,670
engineering. Thanks for watching!
